package jan-klod:interfaces@0.3.0;

/// Run a command as a child process — the execution substrate (Phase 7).
///
/// This is **code execution**, so it is host-owned and bounded: **default-deny**
/// (disabled unless the deployment opts in), the working directory is **jailed to
/// the workspace**, and each run has a **timeout** and an **output cap**. The loop
/// gates it at `tool-call` (`interceptor-permission`).
///
/// Two shapes of child, and the second is **narrower** than the first. `exec`
/// runs a command to completion and is gated by `execution.enabled`. `spawn`
/// holds a child open — what a stdio MCP server, an `lsp-*` or a browser driver
/// needs — and is gated by `execution.long-lived`, which names the processes a
/// guest may start rather than permitting spawning. A guest with the second
/// grant can start what an operator wrote down and nothing else.
///
/// Implemented by the host; imported by execution `tool-*` extensions (bash/eval/…)
/// and — for the long-lived half only — by `registry-mcp`, which holds a stdio
/// MCP server open.
interface host-process {
    /// Why an exec failed.
    enum proc-error {
        /// Execution is disabled, or the `cwd` escapes the workspace.
        denied,
        /// The command exceeded its time budget and was killed.
        timeout,
        /// The command could not be spawned (not found, permissions).
        spawn-failed,
    }

    /// The outcome of a completed command.
    record exit {
        /// Exit code, or -1 if terminated by a signal.
        code: s32,
        /// Captured stdout (truncated to the output cap).
        stdout: string,
        /// Captured stderr (truncated to the output cap).
        stderr: string,
    }

    /// Run `command` with `args`, an optional workspace-relative `cwd`, and optional
    /// `stdin`. Returns the exit code + captured output, or a `proc-error`.
    exec: func(
        command: string,
        args: list<string>,
        cwd: option<string>,
        stdin: option<string>,
    ) -> result<exit, proc-error>;

    /// Opaque handle to a running long-lived child.
    ///
    /// A `u32` rather than a `resource`, matching `llm-provider`'s
    /// `stream-handle` and `agent-delegate`'s `delegate-handle`. The host owns
    /// the child's lifetime either way — it must leave no orphan when the
    /// owning instance stops or the runtime drops — so a resource would tidy
    /// the guest-visible lifetime without discharging that, and the table the
    /// host keeps to enforce it is the same table that validates a handle.
    /// Handles are per-instance, so a forged integer reaches only the forger's
    /// own children.
    type child-handle = u32;

    /// The long-lived children this deployment granted, by name.
    ///
    /// Empty means none, which is the default. A guest could already learn
    /// this by asking for names one at a time and reading the refusals, so
    /// this discloses nothing new — it makes discovery possible without a
    /// guess, which is what a *tool* needs: a model that cannot see the
    /// names cannot ask for one, and a refusal that cannot say what is
    /// available reads as a dead end rather than as a wrong name (#220).
    ///
    /// Names only. The command and arguments behind a name stay the host's,
    /// because that is the whole of what makes this grant narrower than
    /// `execution.enabled`.
    granted: func() -> list<string>;

    /// Start the long-lived child **named** in `execution.long-lived`.
    ///
    /// The name is the whole of the guest's say: the command and its arguments
    /// come from config, so a guest can start a process an operator wrote down
    /// and no other. A name the policy does not carry is `denied`, with the
    /// reason in the host's log — `proc-error` carries no payload, and `exec`
    /// already answers that way.
    ///
    /// The child is confined by the same sandbox backend as a one-shot command.
    /// One that outlives its call is more exposed than one that does not, not
    /// less.
    spawn: func(name: string) -> result<child-handle, proc-error>;

    /// Write to the child's stdin. `denied` if the handle is not one of this
    /// instance's running children.
    write-stdin: func(child: child-handle, data: string) -> result<_, proc-error>;

    /// Read at most `max-bytes` from the child's stdout, waiting up to
    /// `timeout-ms` for the first byte.
    ///
    /// An **empty string means nothing arrived in that window**, not that the
    /// child is finished — ask `is-running` for that. The two are different
    /// questions and a caller that conflates them will either spin or stop
    /// early.
    read-stdout: func(
        child: child-handle,
        max-bytes: u32,
        timeout-ms: u32,
    ) -> result<string, proc-error>;

    /// Whether the child is still alive. `false` for a handle that was never
    /// valid, so a caller polling a killed child terminates rather than
    /// erroring forever.
    is-running: func(child: child-handle) -> bool;

    /// Kill the child and release the handle. Idempotent, and not required for
    /// correctness: the host kills what is left when the instance stops.
    kill: func(child: child-handle);
}
